t on that diagonal. Wait, e6 to f8 would be one up and two right? No. Wait, e6 to f8: e to f is +1 file, 6 to 8 is +2 ranks. So it's a diagonal of two squares up and one right? No, wait, Bishop moves same number of squares in both directions. So e6 to f7 is one up one right, f7 to g8 is another. So e6 to g8 is two up two right. So f8 would be one up from e7, but perhaps not on the same diagonal.\n\nWait, the square f8: what's attacking it? Let's see. Are there any White pieces that can attack f8?\n\nWhite has a King at e4. Can the White King attack f8? No, too far. The White Bishop is on e6, which is on the same diagonal as g8, but f8 is not on that diagonal. Let's calculate:\n\nThe diagonal from e6 (which is e-file, 6th rank) going up-left would be d7, c8. Up-right would be f7, g8. So the Bishop on e6 attacks g8 (as we saw), but f8 is not on that diagonal. So moving King to f8 would get out of check.\n\nSimilarly, g7: is g7 attacked? Let's see. From e6, the Bishop's diagonal to g8 passes through f7. So g7 is not on that diagonal. Wait, e6 to f7 to g8. So g7 is one square below g8. So e6 to g7 would be two up and one right? No, e6 to g7 is two files right (e to g) and one rank up (6 to 7). So that's not a diagonal. So Bishop on e6 doesn't attack g7. What other pieces could attack g7?\n\nWhite King at e4. The distance from e4 to g7 is two files right and three ranks up. Not a King move. So King can't attack g7. Are there other White pieces? Let's see:\n\nWhite has pawns on a3, h3 (PWF1), b2, g2 (PWF0). Pawns move forward, so White pawns are moving up. The pawn on b2 could potentially attack c3, but not relevant here. Similarly, pawns on a3 and h3 are on rank 3, so their attack squares would be rank 4. So no pawns attacking g7.\n\nSo moving King to g7 would be safe.\n\nSimilarly, h8: is h8 attacked? The Bishop on e6's diagonal ends at g8, so h8 is next to g8. Not on the same diagonal. So h8 should be safe.\n\nWait, but wait: the White King is at e4. Could it be that t